{"author":{"id":"8022379585b4ab99ca8775db3eb67d33fcb256817114c28e51ccc1e9f55cdec4"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1792310711,"nonce":"gMgnPCfCqRYZPlOpzEIm8CVh3Es=","title":"FIXED f0235179. One `static var` read as TWO storages: GlobalLoadCachingPass judged a global read-only by scanning only the LOADING ...","message":"It collected `stored_globals` from **the loading function only**, then treated\nany global not stored *there* as read-only and CSE'd its loads. A store done by\na CALLEE was never considered. So in\n\n    buf = grow(buf, n)        // the store is inside grow()\n\nthe caller saw a global it never wrote, cached the pre-call load, and reused it\nfor every later read. The static held its initial value forever from one method\nwhile the method that assigned it saw the new one — silently, no diagnostic.\n\nRead-only now means never stored ANYWHERE in the module, **and** a call is a\nclobber barrier on its own. Both halves are needed: the module-wide set alone\nis insufficient because imported globals are merged into the table, so a store\nmade by the DEFINING module is not among this module's functions. With calls\npresent, forwarding is limited to straight-line reuse inside one block with no\ncall between the two loads; call-free functions keep dominator-based forwarding.\n\n`Q4Matmul._w16`, a 112 MB f16 prefill scratch: `amxPrefillHaxe` assigns it and\nreads back non-null, `matmul` read null on ALL 12,000+ decode calls of a run,\nso it could never be released. **This also RETRACTS the earlier conclusion that\nthe Haxe AMX path \"never allocates\"** (the reason 867df20f was reverted in\n2fa7ac6a) — it does allocate; the write simply was not visible.\n\n1. the writer in its own **MODULE** (inlined, the caller stores the global\n   itself, which the pass already excluded correctly),\n2. a **POINTER-typed** static — an Int static did NOT reproduce, at all,\n3. **both reads inside ONE method called twice**, so the reader ends up holding\n   two loads with the mutating call between them. A reader split into separate\n   accessor functions does not reproduce (nothing to CSE in the caller).\nAlso: `rayzor run Main.hx` will not resolve a sibling module — the repro needs\na `rayzor.toml` with `class-paths`, or the bundle path.\nTest: `compiler/tests/haxe/static_callee_store/` (rayzor.toml + pkg/Holder.hx +\nMain.hx). VERIFIED to fail without the fix (exit 1) and pass with it, on both\nthe LLVM and baseline tiers.\n\n- Chasing this needed FOUR narrowing steps, each cheap: same-file (works) -\u003e\n  cross-module (E0100, blocked) -\u003e rayzor.toml classpath (reproduces) -\u003e\n  direct-assign vs assign-from-call (isolates the trigger). Do not stop at the\n  first minimal case that behaves correctly; vary ONE axis at a time.\n- `RAYZOR_GLOBALS_DEBUG=1`, `RAYZOR_DUMP_MIR=1` and `RAYZOR_DUMP_LLVM_IR=1`\n  (writes /tmp/rayzor_llvm_ir.ll + _opt.ll) are the tools. The MIR was CORRECT\n  in both copies — the damage was done by a later pass, so dumping MIR alone\n  would have cleared the compiler wrongly.\n- A regression test is worthless until you watch it FAIL. Three earlier drafts\n  of this test passed against the unfixed compiler; only the exact shape above\n  has teeth.\n- `cargo test -p compiler --lib` does NOT link at HEAD (pre-existing, fails the\n  same with the file reverted). The crate is named `compiler`, not\n  `rayzor-compiler`.\n\nMistral-7B gate, quiet box: decode 20.23 tok/s vs llama.cpp tg128 17.97,\nprefill 47.60 vs pp512 49.92, peak 4845 MB (4870 before).\nSame id-space family as [[bugs_stdtypes_type_addition_breaks_nue]].\n\nAn audit for siblings of this bug found `CSEPass` doing the same thing WORSE.\nIt keys a global load as `loadglobal:{id}` and the driver walked a block with a\nsingle `available` map that had NO arm for `StoreGlobal` and none for calls. So\nit never asked the read-only question at all, and therefore also fired on the\nplain store-between-two-reads case in straight-line code, which\nGlobalLoadCachingPass could never touch:\n\n    Counter.c = 7; var a = Counter.c; Counter.c = a + 1; var b = Counter.c;\n    a + b   -\u003e  14, must be 15\n\nDefault optimisation level, every backend (the damage is in MIR before a tier\nis chosen). Verified by execution before and after: 14 -\u003e 15, and the\ncallee-store shape 2 -\u003e 3. Fix mirrors this one: a store drops that global's\nkey, any call drops every global key. Pure BinOp/UnOp/Cmp/Cast keys still\nsurvive calls, and the test asserts that repeated reads with NO intervening\nwrite still collapse, so the fix cannot be \"corrected\" into disabling CSE.\nTest: `compiler/tests/haxe/test_global_cse_invalidation.hx` (single file — the\nstore-between shape needs no module split, unlike the one above).\n\n**THE AUDIT IS INCOMPLETE.** 11 of its verifier agents died on a session limit.\nUnverified candidates remain in LICM, ScalarReplacement, Devirtualization,\nBoundsCheckElimination and InsertFree — re-run the audit before assuming the\nfamily is closed. The pattern to grep for: a pass answering a whole-program\nquestion (is this global written? is this call pure? does this pointer escape?)\nfrom a single-function scan.\n\n---\nArchived from Claude memory `bugs_static_var_two_storages_same_class` on 2026-10-18. A record of past work; open items in it may have their own issues.","files":null}]}